      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "It's possible that you're dealing with tomato hornworms, which are moth larvae and can have a lot of blue-green stripes. They usually hide under leaves but can be active at night. However, another possibility is flea beetles, which can be more active during the day and look like they're nibbled. Try a thorough inspection of the undersides of leaves and check the stalks for any tiny caterpillars. A good soap spray might help deter them, but for flea beetles, try using floating row covers to protect the plants.",
